Spec: error

Purpose:
  Own the diagnostic API's error vocabulary: the @DiagError enum whose seven client-visible
    variants carry a stable `DIAG-00X` id and whose four internal variants collapse into
    `DIAG-500`, the per-variant @axum::http::StatusCode mapping, the
    @axum::response::IntoResponse impl that renders every error as a JSON `{code,message}` body
    with that status, and the @internal_error builder the crate uses to raise internal failures.

Owns:
  ./error.rs

Must:
  Each of the seven unit variants embeds its own literal id `DIAG-001` … `DIAG-007` as the
    prefix of its @Display message, and @DiagError::code returns exactly that id for the same
    variant, so wire and log text share the identity for those seven; @DiagError::InternalError
    embeds `DIAG-500` in its template, while @DiagError::KubeError,
    @DiagError::SerializationError and @DiagError::YamlError render no id at all (see Tasks).
  Payload text never drops: @DiagError::InternalError interpolates its @{0} and each of the
    three source wrappers interpolates the wrapped error's @Display through `{0}`, so
    @DiagError::into_response sends caller- and cluster-supplied detail verbatim in the body
    `message`.
  @DiagError::code and @DiagError::status_code are total `pub const fn`s over every variant,
    match on the variant only, and read no payload value.
  @DiagError::into_response consumes @self by value and always answers with the
    @DiagError::status_code status plus an @axum::Json body built from the private internal
    @ErrorResponse: exactly two @String keys in declaration order, `code` (the
    @DiagError::code as @String) and `message` (the full @Display) — no branch yields another
    status source, an empty body, or a non-JSON content type.
  The @From conversions generated by the `#[from]` derives are the only inbound conversions;
    the derives are @Debug and @thiserror::Error only, so @DiagError never deserializes a
    wire error back.
  The module reaches consumers through `pub mod error;` and the crate-root re-export
    @crate::DiagError in ./lib.rs; the re-export itself is owned there.
  The file logs nothing — no tracing or logging call exists here — so the stable-id-to-log-line
    discipline inherited from /vynil.sdd depends entirely on callers rendering @Display or the
    body `code`.

Depends on:
  @axum `0.8` in /server/Cargo.toml — @axum::Json for the body and content type,
    @axum::http::StatusCode for the mapping, and @axum::response::IntoResponse with
    @axum::response::Response for the impl this file completes.
  @thiserror `2` — the @Error derive generating every @Display template and @source chain from
    the `#[error(...)]` and `#[from]` attributes.
  @serde::Serialize — the derive on the internal @ErrorResponse that fixes the JSON keys.
  /server/Cargo.toml — also declaring @kube, @serde_json and @serde_yaml, the error types
    wrapped by @DiagError::KubeError, @DiagError::SerializationError and @DiagError::YamlError.

Exposes:
  @DiagError — `pub enum` deriving @Debug and @thiserror::Error; the crate's only API error
    lane; variant and @Display template in full:
    @DiagError::AuthorizationDenied — unit; `DIAG-001: Authorization denied -
      SubjectAccessReview refused access`
    @DiagError::InstanceNotFound — unit; `DIAG-002: Instance not found`
    @DiagError::InvalidNameFormat — unit; `DIAG-003: Invalid namespace or name format`
    @DiagError::UnknownKind — unit; `DIAG-004: Unknown resource kind`
    @DiagError::UnknownItem — unit; `DIAG-005: Unknown diagnostic item`
    @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired — unit; `DIAG-006: Authentication required - no valid
      identity provided`
    @DiagError::PackagesDisabled — unit; `DIAG-007: Packages endpoint is disabled`
    @DiagError::InternalError(@String) — `DIAG-500: Internal server error - {0}`
    @DiagError::KubeError(@kube::Error, `#[from]`) — `Kubernetes API error: {0}`
    @DiagError::SerializationError(@serde_json::Error, `#[from]`) — `Serialization error: {0}`
    @DiagError::YamlError(@serde_yaml::Error, `#[from]`) — `YAML error: {0}`
  @DiagError::code — `pub const fn(&self)` returning `&'static @str`; one of the eight ids
    listed in Returns.
  @DiagError::status_code — `pub const fn(&self)` returning @axum::http::StatusCode; mapped in
    Returns.
  @IntoResponse for @DiagError — @axum::response::IntoResponse impl taking @self by value and
    returning @axum::response::Response; the file's single error-to-HTTP rendering.
  @internal_error — `pub fn(msg: impl @Into<@String>) -> @DiagError`; constructs
    @DiagError::InternalError with just `msg.into()`, the designated internal-error builder —
    @ErrorResponse itself stays private and is not exposed.

Accepts:
  Construction inputs: @internal_error accepts any `impl @Into<@String>` detail — typically
    @format! text embedding an inner error — with no length or content limit;
    @DiagError::InternalError accepts the same as @String; the seven unit variants take no
    payload.
  Conversions: a @kube::Error, @serde_json::Error or @serde_yaml::Error converts infallibly via
    the derived @From into its named wrapper, so `?` in any @Result<@T, @DiagError> leg is
    exhaustively covered by these three plus hand-built variants.
  @DiagError::into_response accepts every @DiagError by value; the match is exhaustive with no
    precondition.

Returns:
  @DiagError::code: `DIAG-001` @DiagError::AuthorizationDenied, `DIAG-002`
    @DiagError::InstanceNotFound, `DIAG-003` @DiagError::InvalidNameFormat, `DIAG-004`
    @DiagError::UnknownKind, `DIAG-005` @DiagError::UnknownItem, `DIAG-006`
    @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired, `DIAG-007` @DiagError::PackagesDisabled, and `DIAG-500`
    for all four of @DiagError::InternalError, @DiagError::KubeError,
    @DiagError::SerializationError and @DiagError::YamlError.
  @DiagError::status_code: `400` (@StatusCode::BAD_REQUEST) InvalidNameFormat, `401`
    (@StatusCode::UNAUTHORIZED) AuthenticationRequired, `403` (@StatusCode::FORBIDDEN)
    AuthorizationDenied, `404` (@StatusCode::NOT_FOUND) for InstanceNotFound, UnknownKind,
    UnknownItem and PackagesDisabled, and `500` (@StatusCode::INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR) for the
    four internal variants.
  @DiagError::into_response returns a @axum::response::Response with status =
    @DiagError::status_code and an `application/json` body carrying the two keys `code` and
    `message`; because `message` is the @Display, the id appears twice on the wire for the
    seven unit variants and @DiagError::InternalError and only in `code` for the three source
    wrappers.

Raises:
  Nothing — the derives, both `const fn`s and @DiagError::into_response are total and
    infallible; this file itself has no failure or panic path.
  The failure set this vocabulary defines is the eleven variants: client conditions landing at
    `400`/`401`/`403`/`404` (the seven unit variants) and internal conditions collapsed at
    `500` (the four payload variants); no @Result alias or fallback is defined here.

Handles:
  Kubernetes API failures of any kind — including a Kubernetes-side `404` — are handled as
    `500`/`DIAG-500` without inspecting the wrapped status (see Tasks).
  A disabled packages endpoint is handled as a `404`, the same status as the unknown-instance
    family and distinguishable only by the id.
  Payload-bearing variants — @DiagError::code and @DiagError::status_code ignore the payload
    entirely; the payload reaches the client only through @Display and the body `message`.

Tasks:
  [ ] Cover @DiagError::code and @DiagError::status_code across all eleven variants to pin the
      Returns tables.
  [ ] Cover the eleven @Display templates verbatim, including the `DIAG-500` interpolated body
      of @DiagError::InternalError and the inner-error renders of the three wrappers.
  [ ] Cover @IntoResponse: status passthrough, `application/json`, and a body of exactly the
      two keys `code` then `message` with `message` equal to the @Display.
  [ ] Cover the derived @From conversions: `?` on each of @kube::Error, @serde_json::Error and
      @serde_yaml::Error lands in the named variant with the source chain intact.
  [ ] Cover @internal_error producing the same error as direct @DiagError::InternalError
      construction, including an already-@String `msg`.
  [?] @DiagError::KubeError, @DiagError::SerializationError and @DiagError::YamlError render no
      `DIAG-` id in their @Display although @DiagError::code calls them `DIAG-500`, so the id
      reaches the wire only through the body `code` and a logged Display line carries no
      identifier — at odds with "every product error carries a stable, unique identifier in its
      message" in /vynil.sdd. Decide later whether the id moves into those three templates.
  [?] `DIAG-500` is shared by the four internal variants, so the id alone maps no log line to
      one origin inside the `500` lane; only the message prose separates caller text, kube,
      JSON and YAML failures. Decide later whether the lane splits into distinct ids.
  [?] Every @kube::Error — even a Kubernetes `404` for a genuinely missing object — surfaces as
      `500`/`DIAG-500`; the client-facing 404s arise only from hand-built
      @DiagError::InstanceNotFound and siblings elsewhere. Decide later whether
      @DiagError::KubeError should mirror the wrapped status.
  [?] Body `message` on `500` responses carries caller- and cluster-controlled detail verbatim —
      @DiagError::InternalError payload or the wrapped @Display — shipped back to the requesting
      client. Decide later whether `500` bodies become generic with detail log-side only.
  [?] @DiagError::PackagesDisabled surfaces as `404`, indistinguishable in status from an
      unknown instance; the in-file comment pins that choice deliberately. Decide later whether
      the disabled endpoint keeps `404` or takes a distinct status.

Done when:
  Every Scenario below maps to a green test in `server`, the `[?]` decisions above are taken,
    and `cargo test -p server` is green.

Scenario: Unit variants carry distinct ids and statuses
  Given one value of each @DiagError::AuthorizationDenied, @DiagError::InstanceNotFound,
    @DiagError::InvalidNameFormat, @DiagError::UnknownKind, @DiagError::UnknownItem,
    @DiagError::AuthenticationRequired and @DiagError::PackagesDisabled
  When @DiagError::code, @DiagError::status_code and @Display are read
  Then the codes are `DIAG-001` … `DIAG-007` in declaration order, the statuses are `403`,
    `404`, `400`, `404`, `404`, `401` and `404` respectively
  And each @Display message begins with the same id @DiagError::code returns
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Internal lane collapses to DIAG-500
  Given @DiagError::InternalError holding `probe` and one value each of
    @DiagError::KubeError, @DiagError::SerializationError and @DiagError::YamlError
  When @DiagError::code and @DiagError::status_code are read
  Then all four return `DIAG-500` and `500`
  And no payload choice changes either result
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Error becomes a JSON error response
  Given @DiagError::InvalidNameFormat
  When it is consumed through @IntoResponse
  Then the status is `400` and the JSON body is
    `{"code":"DIAG-003","message":"DIAG-003: Invalid namespace or name format"}`
  And on every variant the `message` equals that variant's @Display and the status equals its
    @DiagError::status_code
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: internal_error builds a carrier of caller text
  Given @internal_error called with `Failed to create kube client: error`
  When its @Display and its @IntoResponse body are examined
  Then the @Display is `DIAG-500: Internal server error - Failed to create kube client: error`
    and responds `500` with `code` `DIAG-500`
  And the helper yields the same error as direct @DiagError::InternalError construction
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Source errors enter through ? and answer 500
  Given a function returning @Result<@T, @DiagError> applying `?` to a @kube::Error, a
    @serde_json::Error and a @serde_yaml::Error
  When each propagates
  Then they arrive as @DiagError::KubeError, @DiagError::SerializationError and
    @DiagError::YamlError with their sources recoverable through @std::error::Error::source
  And the client sees `500` with a `message` of `Kubernetes API error: …`-shape carrying no
    `DIAG-` id while `code` says `DIAG-500`
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  @DiagError::AuthorizationDenied body:
    `{"code":"DIAG-001","message":"DIAG-001: Authorization denied - SubjectAccessReview refused access"}`
  @internal_error("TLS config error: no client CA") body:
    `{"code":"DIAG-500","message":"DIAG-500: Internal server error - TLS config error: no client CA"}`
  Wrapper body shape: `{"code":"DIAG-500","message":"Serialization error: ..."}` — the id is on
    the wire but absent from `message`.
